<p>Nolanville is a small but growing city on the US-190 corridor, now also signed as I-14, between Belton and Harker Heights. It is close enough to Fort Cavazos and Killeen that many residents work there, while others commute east toward Temple. Our team drives out from Temple along the same highway, so Nolanville is well within our regular service area.</p>

<h2>Nolanville's Housing Mix</h2>
<p>Much of Nolanville's housing has been built in recent decades, with newer subdivisions alongside older homes and properties on larger lots toward the edges of town. That mix shapes the questions we hear.</p>

<h3>Newer homes</h3>
<p>Owners of newer homes are often surprised that a roof only a few years old can need work. Hail does not care how old a roof is, and builder-grade installations sometimes have weak points like short-nailed shingles, missing kickout flashing at roof-to-wall joints, or too little attic ventilation. A free inspection after a storm can tell you whether there is damage worth documenting while it is still fresh. If you live in an HOA neighborhood, check with your HOA about approved shingle colors before replacing.</p>

<h3>Established homes and rentals</h3>
<p>Older homes in and around town may be on their original or second roof. With Fort Cavazos nearby, many homes here are rentals or belong to military families who may relocate. Owners who move away often want a trustworthy local roofer who can inspect, photograph and report back. We can do that.</p>

<h2>Weather on the US-190 Corridor</h2>
<p>Western Bell County sees the same severe storm patterns as Temple, with spring as the peak season for hail and damaging wind and a second round possible in the fall. The terrain begins to roll and rise toward the west, and homes on higher or more open ground catch more wind. Summer heat is relentless, and attics in newer homes with minimal ventilation can get extremely hot, which ages shingles from below. Our article on <a href="/blog/texas-summer-heat-roof-attic-ventilation/">Texas summer heat and attic ventilation</a> explains why that matters.</p>

